{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1211728-how-do-you-design-a-story","id":1211728,"title":"How do you design a story?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Exalted"],"posts":[{"date":"2018-04-26T03:39:21-07:00","id":1211728,"title":"How do you design a story?","content":"Hi crowd. I've been wondering about this problem since i started organizing a EX3 game. You can't very well do dungeon runs and orc invasions in EX3 like you can in D&amp;D, even if you make them First Age Tombs and Fae incursions. Beyond &quot;Go get the Mcguffin&quot;, what kind of stories are you guys running that take advantage of the world/mechanics of Exalted?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Please be warned: this is not champagne, this is most likely a duck.<br /> -<i>Chausse</i><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#800080\"><i>Message me for Japanese translations.</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8830-sorcerous-overlord","name":"Sorcerous Overlord","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8830_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"873"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-26T04:37:18-07:00","id":1211737,"title":"","content":"...You don't.<br /> <br /> You write characters who:<br /> - have motivations the PCs can understand and empathize with<br /> - feel strongly enough about those motivations that they're willing to do extreme things in pursuit of them<br /> - and are, as a consequence, brought into conflict with other characters you wrote in the same manner.<br /> <br /> Then you watch the ensuing fireworks as multiple sides go against one another, with the PCs' actions ultimately deciding who comes out on top. If you did your prep work right, a story falls out of the resulting mess.<br /> <br /> There are nuances to the process but I really want to get some food now, so we'll leave it at that for now.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/823884-3e-the-artifact-and-evocation-workshop?p=943303#post943303\" target=\"_blank\">Evocations for the demonic tattoos gained from the Pact with Mara sorcerous initiation</a> || <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/823884-3e-the-artifact-and-evocation-workshop?p=907071#post907071\" target=\"_blank\">Pyre-Kindler (Soulsteel and Red Jade Grimscythe, Artifact 3)</a> || <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1072019-soulsteel-stormcaller-concept-gravedigger?p=1074897#post1074897\" target=\"_blank\">Tenebrous Descent (Stormcaller's Black Jade Reaver Daiklave cousin, Artifact 5)</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1074256-combat-with-corebook-shikari-antagonists?p=1074303#post1074303\" target=\"_blank\">Advice for running the corebook shikari antagonists</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3443-aluminiumtrioxid","name":"aluminiumtrioxid","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3443_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"606"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-26T04:52:13-07:00","id":1211741,"title":"","content":"One can design a story up to the point players interfere XD<br /> <br /> Which by the most part has to be believable and end in something that engages the players.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/32-synapse/visitormessage/1408635-visitor-message-from-synapse#post1408635\" target=\"_blank\">Check my Exalted homebrew</a>!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/32-synapse","name":"Synapse","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar32_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1507"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-26T05:07:43-07:00","id":1211744,"title":"","content":"Be willing to accept input from your players. Can't emphasize that enough.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-26T05:29:29-07:00","id":1211752,"title":"","content":"As they said. You don\u00b4t. I think you can do it for other games, but Exalted is... too open-ended, and the characters have too much power, freedom and agency to be able to design a story and hope the players follow it.<br /> <br /> Anyway, that\u00b4s cool. The best story you can tell is the one which emerges in play, in which both you and all of your players are interested. That\u00b4s the reason that my group and me always choose the campaign concept colaboratively, or at least wait until they choose their objectives/create their characters before working on the chronicle.<br /> <br /> What I do, and what I have found usually works, is creating a setting. Design the place where you will be playing (from a city, for a very local game, to a collection of kingdoms and tribes and what have you for a more expansive game), populate it with cool conflicts, and places, and NPCs, and mix them. Before you notice, the interaction between players and NPCs (or the logical interactions between the NPCs themselves) will start giving you plot ideas, and knowing what to do. Try not to write isolated NPCs: Okay, Cynis Talora and Hundred Heavens Bride may both be cool characters, awesome even, but if they don\u00b4t have conflicts with other characters in the setting, or don\u00b4t have passionate, obsessive objectives, or don\u00b4t want anything from the players, they won\u00b4t be useful for the game. Passionate, driven characters are what, well, drive a game.<br /> <br /> At most, generate a general situation, and let the story flow from that. Don\u00b4t make your chronicle about how the players unify the Fifteen Tribes of Khaldor, recover the Rock-Crusher and use them to drive back the Realm from their region. Make it about how the Realm is invading Khaldor, the home-country of the players. Do the players unify the Tribes, hoping to be able to defend their country from the imperialists? Do they quests for a ground-shattering weapon, missheard from ancient tales? Do they betray their peoples and join the Realm, thinking that this could avoid bloodshed, and better be humiliated than dead? Do they do that, to later betray the Realm again? Do they lose from the beginning, forced to become a guerrilla movement, and later search for Dark Powers to which sell their souls in exchange of the power to free their people? I don\u00b4t know what they will do, but what I know is that it probably will be awesome, and a good story.<br /> <br /> Build a place. Be sure it is awesome, and full of awesome people. Steal from other sources if you need. Trust in your players, I am sure they are awesome. Enjoy. <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13031-clophiroth","name":"Clophiroth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13031_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"293"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-26T06:02:40-07:00","id":1211759,"title":"","content":"I set up a basic adventure well aware that Exalted PCs have shortcuts and means to shrug off otherwise certain death.<br /> When it's over I ask &quot;then what?&quot; and keep asking that as they dig themselves deeper and deeper into trouble adding in new adventures every so often, most notably when a new PC gets added which can mean sometimes they're riding the success waves of multiple adventures at once.<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Sword of Creation</a> a hub for Exalted related content</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":7}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","name":"Lioness","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7_2.png","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5443"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-26T08:28:25-07:00","id":1211817,"title":"","content":"* Talk to your players about what kinds of stories they are interested in. High action? Nation building? Wandering heroes righting wrongs?<br /> <br /> * Determine what the current status quo is. The status quo should be something that either the players and their characters will want to challenge, or will want to protect.<br /> <br /> * Create at least four NPCs; two that overtly benefit from and support the status quo, and two that overtly suffer from and would change the status quo. Then, secretly change one of the supporters of the status quo to someone who wants to change it (either out of principle or for personal gain or a tie or whatever), and one of the detractors into a secret supporter (again- either out of principle, or because they covertly benefit from the status quo in a non-obvious way, or out of fear that change might bring something worse).<br /> <br /> * Entangle the PCs; give them a tie which is at odds with their other intimacies (friendship with a noble that is oppressing the people, hired to deal with bandits that are actually righteous rebels, etc).<br /> <br /> * Shake well and stand back.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>My Storyteller Vault releases!<br /> <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/269728/Sorcerous-Ways-Mystic-Martial-Arts\" target=\"_blank\">Sorcerous Ways: Mystic Martial Arts</a> - Punch people for magic power!<br /> <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/252892/These-Mean-Streets\" target=\"_blank\">These Mean Streets</a> - Advise and systems for street-level punk-noir Vampire larp","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/169-wastevens","name":"wastevens","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar169_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"939"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-26T08:56:48-07:00","id":1211832,"title":"","content":"Of the styles listed so far, mine most closely resembles that of Lioness. I typically have a set end point for the story. I think a lot of this discussion revolves on the differences between Serialized/Episodic play and set length narrative. Both can work, but player and ST expectations need to be discussed beforehand, and it\u2019s absolutely critical to have player buy in before attempting a long form narrative game. Just my .02","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11967-saipjas","name":"Saipjas","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"270"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-26T09:16:18-07:00","id":1211839,"title":"","content":"I could probably formulate some useful general advice but it's late and I'm feeling rambley, so let me tell you a story ... <br /> <br /> When I first played Exalted, the game started out with a local scope, based in Nexus. We built connections to the local authorities, expanded our influence, built up our base (we had a mercenary company), took part in local politics, tried to make the city a better place while advancing our own interests. It was great. We felt a real sense of ownership of what we were doing, the ST was great at challenging us with new scenarios and rolling with the consequences of our actions to come up with more challenges, and our version of Nexus developed a real sense of place and character of its own. Our characters had lives there, despite being called to adventure. We started testing the waters beyond Nexus and considering our long-term goals. <br /> <br /> Then it changed. We were pulled out of that local scope, before we were ready, and thrust into the tumultuous sea of world-spanning plots known &quot;affectionately&quot; in the fandom as the Thousand Dooms. Gone was the relevance of our previous efforts on the local stage, gone was any sense of exploration as we were jerked across Creation from one urgent crisis to another with no time to breathe or reflect. We were doing more &quot;epic&quot; and &quot;badass&quot; things than before, but the game was lacking its earlier charm. <br /> <br /> When it came time for me to design my own campaign, I knew I wanted that sense of place and character for the little slice of the setting the PCs would call home, and for my players to have that sense of ownership over their goals. I also knew I was in trouble, because I wanted to use several Creation-spanning threats as major plot elements. So I set out to tame the Thousand Dooms.<ol class=\"decimal\"><li>I knew I wanted to include the legions of Hell, the fae hordes, and the machinations of the deathlords as pressing threats to Creation, but I didn't want these threats to stifle the players with their urgency, so I stretched out the count-down so that these threats would become overwhelming in a matter of decades, not months or years. To do this I gave Creation defences that could hold the bulk of their strength at bay, but which were slowly failing.</li> <li>I wanted the threats to be in progress when the game began, so in order that the players not be overwhelmed by the urgency and scope of the threats, I made sure that they didn't know about them. Not because the Dooms were biding their time, but because the conflicts were actively hidden from most of the world. The battle for Creation was playing out as a secret war that the PCs could discover and get involved in, with each faction vying for the decisive opportunity they needed to save the world or end everything.</li> <li>In order to complement the &quot;vast conspiracy&quot; storyline and facilitate the sort of world-trotting that facing down the Thousand Dooms tends to call for, I decided on a modern setting. It's also easier to have down-time at home between missions when travel doesn't take weeks.</li> <li>Having deprived the Dooms of their terrible, all-consuming urgency, I ensured they still had teeth by dialing up the horror. In this case, the horror was inherent not only in what Creation's enemies were up to but what Creation's defenders had already had to do to prevent the apocalypse, and the fact that their blasphemous sacrifice had only been enough to buy them time. The first Act consisted of the PCs finding their feet and pursuing their own goals while slowly uncovering the Horrible Truth (tm).</li> <li>Since the threat to Creation was horrific but not imminent, the PCs had time to explore what was going on and decide how to approach the situation, seeking allies, contacts, artifacts etc, stepping onto the world stage at their own pace on their own terms, for the most part. Since the threat was hidden from the general population, life in Creation went on, and so their lives went on as well. They had to juggle friendships, families and careers around their growing involvement with the war. They had time to pursue agendas not related to the looming apocalypses competing to destroy Creation, and to help each other achieve these goals (when not accidentally hindering each other instead).</li> </ol>And it's worked. One PC has responded to finding out about the Dooms by taking up Crafting to try to build a solution, or at least understand the problem, while staying involved in Tengese politics by spinning a web of espionage around rival houses (that occasionally pulls in the rest of the party). One has gone from being a teenage pit-fighter to a well-respected military officer (though after some rash choices last session her career might be shot to hell). One helped develop a vaccine for a mind-altering virus created by the deathlords, but also got the group involved in taking down a corrupt dragonblooded general whose plots threatened the group of rebels whose cause she had taken up. One of them continues to take the odd case as a PI while also honing his sorcerous skills and magical knowledge to help in the fight, and has spent a lot of time chasing the unanswered questions of his past. Through it all they've built an organisation of their own design that has become instrumental to the war effort. They're movers and shakers in the hidden power structures of Creation, poised to finally put a decisive end to the struggle and save the whole world like the big damn heroes they are - and their personal struggles have never stopped mattering to the story. <br /> <br /> Anyway, the moral of all of this is probably to keep your story grounded in the personal regardless of how grand the scope of the campaign is, or something.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Measure of Hope is right about everything.&quot; - Wise Old Guru<br /> <br /> Currently running an Exalted 2.5 Abyssals game in a homebrew modern shard because I value neither my time or my sanity, and I'm loving almost every minute of it.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23907-measure-of-hope","name":"Measure of Hope","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"190"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-26T09:23:39-07:00","id":1211845,"title":"","content":"You don't.<br /> <br /> You design a setting, and the players write the story through their actions.<br /> <br /> If that sounds difficult.... Yeah, it is. Not gonna lie. But, it is -immensely- satisfying.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-26T09:38:24-07:00","id":1211851,"title":"","content":"Exalted plays very well as a sandbox game. Every single attempt at anything resembling a story has crashed and burned quite quickly.<br /> <br /> Know who is in the area what they want and are doing, events that are going on, and release the players into the sandbox and revel in the glory of the players smashing everyone else's sand castles.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/151-hark","name":"Hark","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar151_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"932"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-26T09:42:05-07:00","id":1211854,"title":"","content":"A lot of the posters here made some good points that I won\u2019t repeat.<br /> <br /> Something that I will say is I\u2019ve had a more recent tendency to come up with a thousand different, unrelated stories. The thing with exalts, Solars especially, is if you have one big problem they tend to just be able to solve it really quickly, regardless of how big that problem is. So to give time for a story with actual growth I tend to run with a big list of locations in my setting and a horrible problem at each location. Then through those adventures my characters tend to evolve and grow and usually keep running into the same NPCs to form bonds there, for better or worse. Plus this also stops the PCs from getting frustrated in a long game with not being able to deal with their real enemy for like 8 sessions, the little victories help.<br /> <br /> Sometimes there might be one big bad thing that sets off most of the little problems, but they\u2019re separated from them by a good chunk. Another thing I do is rather than like low-level DnD where I might put thought into a plan on how to deal with things, in exalted generally I throw a problem at them with zero obvious solutions and let them figure it out.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1121-drlovemonkey","name":"DrLoveMonkey","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1121_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4736"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-26T09:50:33-07:00","id":1211857,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hark</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1211728-how-do-you-design-a-story?p=1211851#post1211851\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Exalted plays very well as a sandbox game. Every single attempt at anything resembling a story has crashed and burned quite quickly.<br /> <br /> Know who is in the area what they want and are doing, events that are going on, and release the players into the sandbox and revel in the glory of the players smashing everyone else's sand castles.</div> </div> </div> </div>I...honestly hearing all of these points made, I guess my play experience is the exception?? I had a narrative that Ran 2.5 full years playing ever other weekend. It was not in anyway a sandbox design as everyone else has been talking, but a full on long form narrative story. So *shrug*. Also the games of Exalted I participated in where fully fleshout narrative games.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11967-saipjas","name":"Saipjas","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"270"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-26T09:56:03-07:00","id":1211859,"title":"","content":"Yeah, in Exalted I like to come up with problems I don't believe are solvable, and watch my players try to solve them. Sometimes they brute Force it with the power sometimes they get clever, and half the time they solve it in a way that creates three new problems. Rinse and repeat.<br /> <br /> Also, I tend to have timelines for what major NPCs are doing in the backyard. The Time of Tumult feels more tumultuous when they're getting news and rumors of wars or coups or religious movements or newly formed shadowland in neighboring countries they haven't visited yet.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>So I'm making <a href=\"http://godkickingboot.smackjeeves.com/\" target=\"_blank\">God-Kicking Boot</a>, an Exalted webcomic, now. Updates on Sundays. Full-color, mediocre but slowly improving art. It's a thing.<br /> <br /> The absence of a monument can, in its own way, be something of a monument also.</i> -Roger Zelazny","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/512-wise-old-guru","name":"Wise Old Guru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar512_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1097"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-26T10:37:18-07:00","id":1211884,"title":"","content":"Joseph Manola's <a href=\"http://udan-adan.blogspot.hu/2016/06/romantic-fantasy-revisited-3-how-do-i.html\" target=\"_blank\">romantic fantasy how-to</a> is also helpful. (It's ostensibly for a specific playstyle in a specific system, but I think it works fine as a GMing best practices guide as well.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/823884-3e-the-artifact-and-evocation-workshop?p=943303#post943303\" target=\"_blank\">Evocations for the demonic tattoos gained from the Pact with Mara sorcerous initiation</a> || <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/823884-3e-the-artifact-and-evocation-workshop?p=907071#post907071\" target=\"_blank\">Pyre-Kindler (Soulsteel and Red Jade Grimscythe, Artifact 3)</a> || <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1072019-soulsteel-stormcaller-concept-gravedigger?p=1074897#post1074897\" target=\"_blank\">Tenebrous Descent (Stormcaller's Black Jade Reaver Daiklave cousin, Artifact 5)</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1074256-combat-with-corebook-shikari-antagonists?p=1074303#post1074303\" target=\"_blank\">Advice for running the corebook shikari antagonists</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3443-aluminiumtrioxid","name":"aluminiumtrioxid","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3443_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"606"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-26T10:43:25-07:00","id":1211886,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>aluminiumtrioxid</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1211728-how-do-you-design-a-story?p=1211884#post1211884\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Joseph Manola's <a href=\"https://&amp;quot;&#91;url\" target=\"_blank\">http://udan-adan.blogspot.hu/2016/06/romantic-fantasy-revisited-3-how-do-i.html&amp;quot;]romantic</a> fantasy how-to[/URL] is also helpful. (It's ostensibly for a specific playstyle in a specific system, but I think it works fine as a GMing best practices guide as well.)</div> </div> </div> </div>That is awesome, some of these are things I\u2019d already had been using in running my own games, but thank you for putting this out there!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11967-saipjas","name":"Saipjas","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"270"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-26T11:32:45-07:00","id":1211908,"title":"","content":"My simple storyline design is as followed. I call it Plot hook style.<br /> <br /> Players make characters - figure out who they are as a person<br /> Create an Antagonist and their goals which include the events which need to be met to complete the task. (I normally include all local antagonist in the area to keep up options and complicate things for the players)<br /> <br /> Set up a location which all players would meet - the First plot hook<br /> Event to tie them all together - second plot hook normally an introduction style<br /> <br /> introduce either the event or Antagonist to meet the end goal of the villain of the story. - The big plot hook<br /> then react to the players until either the players leave the story or all the events have been dealt with.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Like the Sun I am relentless even in death...","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3293-rahod","name":"Rahod","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"310"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-26T11:33:48-07:00","id":1211909,"title":"","content":"It\u2019s actually kind of funny, for all that Solars have run roughshod over my combat encounters they\u2019re surprisingly easy to guide narritively. It\u2019s all about the framing.<br /> <br /> In the first 3e campaign I ran for instance had a story where I wanted the players to go to the south of the kingdom, restore a fortress manse there, and fend off an encroaching army of beast men, saving one of the NOCs they met in the previously.<br /> <br /> So to do this instead of just giving them the info on the beastmen, I framed it as somebody begging for help to restore this manse in order to keep the southern border safe, just so they were more inclined to finish that mission once they\u2019d started it instead of just bowling over the threat.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1121-drlovemonkey","name":"DrLoveMonkey","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1121_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4736"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-27T01:13:37-07:00","id":1212173,"title":"","content":"At first I was a little dismayed at how many of you said &quot;You don't design a Story&quot;, but as I kept reading I kind of get what you guys are talking about. The Exalted are better served by creating a scenario for them and leaving them to cobble together Glorious Solar [Plans]. I'll give that a try. I think my overarching goal for &quot;Season 1&quot; is that the solars are going to try to stop the Deathlords from unleashing a leviathan behemoth. We'll build toward that little by little.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Please be warned: this is not champagne, this is most likely a duck.<br /> -<i>Chausse</i><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#800080\"><i>Message me for Japanese translations.</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8830-sorcerous-overlord","name":"Sorcerous Overlord","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8830_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"873"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-27T02:58:02-07:00","id":1212188,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"https://cleverlintales.wordpress.com/2018/04/27/ryoko-owari-or-how-i-learned-to-stop-worrying-and-love-the-sandbox/\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://s0.wp.com/i/blank.jpg?m=1383295312i\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://cleverlintales.wordpress.com/2018/04/27/ryoko-owari-or-how-i-learned-to-stop-worrying-and-love-the-sandbox/\" target=\"_blank\"> Ryoko Owari or How I Learned to Stop Worrying and Love the Sandbox </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://cleverlintales.wordpress.com/2018/04/27/ryoko-owari-or-how-i-learned-to-stop-worrying-and-love-the-sandbox/\" target=\"_blank\"> https://cleverlintales.wordpress.com/2018/04/27/ryoko-owari-or-how-i-learned-to-stop-worrying-and-love-the-sandbox/ </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> Previous posts focused on mechanics or setting for Exalted, but this time, I will like to focus on something more general: GMing tips. I don&acute;t say they are good tips. Heck, they probably aren&acute;t. Bu&hellip; </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> This conversation and thread inspired me to write a post writing about what changed my gming style to &quot;Ok, build a situation, let players do what they want there, they will carry the plot&quot;, which is what I suggested in this thread","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13031-clophiroth","name":"Clophiroth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13031_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"293"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-27T05:33:02-07:00","id":1212233,"title":"","content":"Oh, while writing up something, a thought occurred to me as regards Storytelling, particularly for those with a background in D&amp;D:<br /> <br /> <i>Don't punish metagaming when it's done in the name of telling a good story</i>.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-27T06:17:03-07:00","id":1212247,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TheCountAlucard</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1211728-how-do-you-design-a-story?p=1212233#post1212233\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Oh, while writing up something, a thought occurred to me as regards Storytelling, particularly for those with a background in D&amp;D:<br /> <br /> <i>Don't punish metagaming when it's done in the name of telling a good story</i>.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I'd go one step further and say &quot;don't punish metagaming, full stop&quot;.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/823884-3e-the-artifact-and-evocation-workshop?p=943303#post943303\" target=\"_blank\">Evocations for the demonic tattoos gained from the Pact with Mara sorcerous initiation</a> || <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/823884-3e-the-artifact-and-evocation-workshop?p=907071#post907071\" target=\"_blank\">Pyre-Kindler (Soulsteel and Red Jade Grimscythe, Artifact 3)</a> || <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1072019-soulsteel-stormcaller-concept-gravedigger?p=1074897#post1074897\" target=\"_blank\">Tenebrous Descent (Stormcaller's Black Jade Reaver Daiklave cousin, Artifact 5)</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1074256-combat-with-corebook-shikari-antagonists?p=1074303#post1074303\" target=\"_blank\">Advice for running the corebook shikari antagonists</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3443-aluminiumtrioxid","name":"aluminiumtrioxid","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3443_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"606"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-27T06:37:28-07:00","id":1212251,"title":"","content":"<b>Example: Okeanos</b><br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\">So my seafaring game started off with me asking the players why their characters would be in the Neck.<br /> <br /> -Barbus' player said, &quot;His cousin the chief sent him on a quest to slay the Frog Queen, so here's where he's gonna start looking for her.&quot; I decided that, standoffish as the Frog Queen is, she is still considered to be a player in the spirit courts here, so it would be a good idea on Barbus' part if he got on the good side of the Ocean Court.<br /> <br /> -Seamstress' player said, &quot;Her father, the Silver Prince, sent her into the lands of the living.&quot; Well, okay; I asked her if she was cool with the Silver Prince having sent her to recover a stolen item of great significance. <br /> <br /> -&quot;Hogane and Hectorae,&quot; their players told me, &quot;just washed up on the shores after sailing from the Demon City, and figured it was as good a place as any to do some honest pirating.&quot; I suggested maybe Hectorae could use his Realm connections to obtain letters of marque. Hogane's player and I also worked out a story in which he had earned the ire of the Lintha Family, which would begin expressing itself as soon as they realized he wasn't dead.<br /> <br /> With everyone having a reason to be there, I then started to cook up a story: the Silver Prince had slain another Deathlord some time back, and had trapped his soul in an amulet. This amulet was stolen by an Abyssal necromancer, Gasp of the Drowning, who then went to the Neck as part of a plan to bring it somewhere the torpid Deathlord could regenerate. <br /> <br /> Gasp split the amulet in half, and put one piece on a ship going to a distant island. The other half, he took with him when he stole the Neck's shipment to the Realm, and nestled it amidst the treasures heaped there as he sailed away. <br /> <br /> So flash forward to the PCs' arrival to Solid Shell. Barbus rapidly figures out that he is going to have trouble making headway with the priests of the Ocean Father without any money, so he begins to look for work. Hogane and Hectorae get their letters of marque, and buy a ship. <br /> <br /> (Side note: this is where Hogane attracted the attention of the Lintha, who laid a curse on him naming him Enemy to Shark-Kind.)<br /> <br /> Hearing of the stolen shipment, the two insist on calling dibs on it, even after hearing about the Anathema pirate, and decide to start hiring crew. Seamstress hears about the Abyssal and figures it's entirely likely to be the same being who stole the amulet, and gets herself hired as the ship's surgeon. Hogane and Hectorae hire Barbus as well, and for some reason decide to make him master of arms. <br /> <br /> <b>Anchors aweigh!</b><br /> <br /> Once the circle starts to catch up to Gasp, he starts resorting to nasty tactics, binding ghosts to spy and commit sabotage and such, befouling their water, and (when he realizes Barbus enjoys the tactic of &quot;swim over to the other boat and knock a hole in it,&quot;) leaving chum in the water behind him to attract sharks. And once the sharks are attracted, they don't lose interest, thanks to Hogane's curse.<br /> <br /> As a result, the crew is starting to get uneasy. Downright paranoid, even. It doesn't help that Hogane outright lies to the crew about the sharks, or that the solution Hectorae advocates for the spectral sabotages is to <i>have the crew punished for it</i>. <br /> <br /> Hectorae continues to be unlikeable; in addition to playing the part of the pompous asshole Dynast, he's also <i>creepy</i>, in that he never sleeps, he brags to people about his ability to murder them, and from time to time, he shows his Caste mark and sets his sword to burning with green hellfire.<br /> <br /> After the fifth straight day of shark attacks, Hogane decides the best course of action is to summon a flying whale for him and Barbus to chase Gasp, while Seamstress and Hectorae stay on the ship. The whale gets savaged by the sharks before it can take off, but Hogane pilots the whale to Gasp's ship.<br /> <br /> Meanwhile, Hectorae elects to go down in the ship's lifeboat with his superheavy armor and sword to kill the sharks still circling their ship; he instructs the crew to man the ballistas and cover him. But not before reminding them all one last time that he could slaughter them all with one hand tied behind his back. <br /> <br /> So naturally once the lifeboat is a decent distance from the ship, the crew ask Seamstress to see to some matter belowdecks, turn the ballista on the lifeboat, and sink the fucker, and no one can say he didn't bring it on himself. <br /> <br /> Hogane and Barbus defeat Gasp in a spectacular battle, with Barbus literally ripping Gasp in half at the waist. Gasp elects to play dead after that. With the ship sinking, Barbus picks up the top half of Gasp's &quot;corpse,&quot; all the treasure, and hauls it back to the whale, while Hogane picks up the ship's log, suspecting it may hold valuable secrets.<br /> <br /> The whale dies of its injuries just in sight of the ship, splashing down next to it in the water, where a triumphant Hogane and Barbus ask what happened to Hectorae.<br /> <br /> &quot;The sharks ate him,&quot; the crew answer, not entirely a lie. <br /> <br /> Seamstress examines the treasure for amulets while Barbus swims down to recover Hectorae's armor, and then everyone makes their way back to the Neck. </div></div> </div> </div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":177,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","user":"TheCountAlucard","date":". 04-27-2018, 09:59 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-27T07:23:37-07:00","id":1212264,"title":"","content":"I have a question for the group here, given the wild divergences between my experience and ya\u2019lls. Are your games open ended or do they last for a set period of time? I ask this because all the exalted games I have played/ran have had a particular end point where play will be concluded.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11967-saipjas","name":"Saipjas","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"270"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-27T09:12:56-07:00","id":1212319,"title":"","content":"My game has a defined end-point. We might wrap up a few loose threads after the main plot concludes (if the PCs survive), but in general I know where the story is headed. That being said, I deliberately structured that story around giving the PCs room to generate their own plot threads based on their personal goals and engage with the &quot;main quest-line&quot; at their own pace and with their own approach. I've never set a time-limit out of game but at our current pace it'll be this year.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Measure of Hope is right about everything.&quot; - Wise Old Guru<br /> <br /> Currently running an Exalted 2.5 Abyssals game in a homebrew modern shard because I value neither my time or my sanity, and I'm loving almost every minute of it.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23907-measure-of-hope","name":"Measure of Hope","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"190"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-27T09:35:12-07:00","id":1212327,"title":"","content":"Unrelated, but <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1007054-after-years-of-being-a-storyteller\">here's</a> some more good advice.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-28T04:28:45-07:00","id":1212595,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>aluminiumtrioxid</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1211728-how-do-you-design-a-story?p=1212247#post1212247\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'd go one step further and say &quot;don't punish metagaming, full stop&quot;.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'd make that &quot;don't punish your players and don't punish yourself&quot;.<br /> I've seen so many GMs hide behind 'actions have consequences' rather than admitting a player's actions pissed them off and trying to resolve it directly. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saipjas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1211728-how-do-you-design-a-story?p=1212264#post1212264\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I have a question for the group here, given the wild divergences between my experience and ya\u2019lls. Are your games open ended or do they last for a set period of time? I ask this because all the exalted games I have played/ran have had a particular end point where play will be concluded.</div> </div> </div> </div>Been running the same game since 2008. I'll die someday I guess.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Sword of Creation</a> a hub for Exalted related content</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","name":"Lioness","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7_2.png","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5443"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-28T06:12:20-07:00","id":1212620,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lioness</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1211728-how-do-you-design-a-story?p=1212595#post1212595\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'd make that &quot;don't punish your players and don't punish yourself&quot;.<br /> I've seen so many GMs hide behind 'actions have consequences' rather than admitting a player's actions pissed them off and trying to resolve it directly.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Definitely agreed. It's kind of amazing how far a philosophy of &quot;don't punish players&quot; can get you - I regularly see people complain that their players are building characters who are impossible to challenge, and the only thing I can think of is &quot;I've never had that problem&quot;. Now, I can't <i>prove </i>a causal relationship between my strident adherence to the idea that players shouldn't be punished for suboptimal in-game decisions or character-building choices, but I've noticed that the players whose primary play experiences were games I ran for them have usually been much more willing to allow their characters to have glaring weak points than the ones whose formative play experiences were in games ran by other people.<br /> <br /> <br /> 'Actions have consequences' is, indeed, a thing I've heard often, but my experience is a bit different than yours - I've mostly heard it from well-meaning GMs, who then, in other discussions, wonder why their players are reluctant to play &quot;the proper way&quot; (refraining from picking up character-defining ratings in skills their character concept does not mandate, investing into merits that make their characters vulnerable, taking actions in play that would fit their character but know would endanger them, et cetera).<br /> <br /> ...At this point, the point I'm trying to make is somewhat running away from me, I'm sorry...<br /> <br /> It would be easy to say something radical like &quot;and from this, we can surmise that the philosophy of 'actions have consequences' is poison even when applied by well-meaning Game Masters&quot;. Or even something like that, but with the addition of &quot;...unless great care is taken&quot;. But that would be wrong. Consistency is important. <i>Verisimilitude </i>is important. These are upheld by enforcing consequences. <br /> <br /> However, at the same time, consequences are on a spectrum. Some are believable, but don't produce fun gameplay. Others help the game move more smoothly, but at the cost of rubbing against the fictional world's believability. But at the same time, you also want to teach your players that <i>it's possible to recover from mistakes</i>. And you have to be mindful of pacing, and the fact that your players will interpret things differently than you do, and...<br /> <br /> ...The point is that games are <i>elastic</i>, tradeoffs have to be weighed against one another, and while realistic consequences are important, they're not necessarily <i>the </i>most important thing to uphold in play. 'Actions have consequences' is one of those memes that have to be applied carefully, even when one means well.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/823884-3e-the-artifact-and-evocation-workshop?p=943303#post943303\" target=\"_blank\">Evocations for the demonic tattoos gained from the Pact with Mara sorcerous initiation</a> || <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/823884-3e-the-artifact-and-evocation-workshop?p=907071#post907071\" target=\"_blank\">Pyre-Kindler (Soulsteel and Red Jade Grimscythe, Artifact 3)</a> || <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1072019-soulsteel-stormcaller-concept-gravedigger?p=1074897#post1074897\" target=\"_blank\">Tenebrous Descent (Stormcaller's Black Jade Reaver Daiklave cousin, Artifact 5)</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1074256-combat-with-corebook-shikari-antagonists?p=1074303#post1074303\" target=\"_blank\">Advice for running the corebook shikari antagonists</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3443-aluminiumtrioxid","name":"aluminiumtrioxid","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3443_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"606"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-05-05T19:17:58-07:00","id":1215649,"title":"","content":"Another nice bit of advice, from a thread that wasn't even about STing.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Murcushio</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/443412-will-factory-cathedrals-be-a-thing-in-3rd-e?p=444323#post444323\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Tangent: I actually love to use [Castle Heterodyne's] plotline as an example of how things that work great in one kind of narrative absolutely don't make the jump over to RPG's well.<br /> <br /> In most games, it is a really, really bad idea for your PCs to spend a long time restoring a place of power, unlocking it's mysteries and unriddling it's intricacies, only for a powerful NPC to drop in when their task is all-but-completely with a giant &quot;No, fuck <i>you</i>&quot; artifact that undoes all their hard work with the click of a button.<br /> <br /> That's fine in an author-controlled narrative. It absolutely doesn't work in a collaborative environment most of the time, especially one where people identify strongly with their characters and expect a certain degree of input into their own success.<br /> <br /> (I have seen a lot of players trying to make the jump to GM fall down in this regard, where they think what they're doing is writing a novel that just happens to have other people providing the dialogue.)</div> </div> </div> </div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":177,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","user":"TheCountAlucard","date":". 05-05-2018, 10:51 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-05-05T19:48:28-07:00","id":1215658,"title":"","content":"That is a good thing to keep in mind! It's also not that you couldn't do that in a game at all, it's just that the understanding that it could or will happen needs to be something communicated with everyone at the table.<br /> <br /> Like an example I can think of where that kind of thing could be a fun thing is if the players maybe feel things have gotten a little stale, or that they want a new direction in the game because both they and the ST are unhappy with how its turned out, and decide to use something like that as an inciting event or soft reboot of things to push the campaign in a new direction.<br /> <br /> It could be a fun start of a campaign too!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Violet Green, Freelancer</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23657-violetdreamer","name":"VioletDreamer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23657_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"163"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-05-05T20:05:22-07:00","id":1215661,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>aluminiumtrioxid</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1211728-how-do-you-design-a-story?p=1212620#post1212620\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> Definitely agreed. It's kind of amazing how far a philosophy of &quot;don't punish players&quot; can get you - I regularly see people complain that their players are building characters who are impossible to challenge, and the only thing I can think of is &quot;I've never had that problem&quot;. Now, I can't <i>prove </i>a causal relationship between my strident adherence to the idea that players shouldn't be punished for suboptimal in-game decisions or character-building choices, but I've noticed that the players whose primary play experiences were games I ran for them have usually been much more willing to allow their characters to have glaring weak points than the ones whose formative play experiences were in games ran by other people.<br /> <br /> <br /> 'Actions have consequences' is, indeed, a thing I've heard often, but my experience is a bit different than yours - I've mostly heard it from well-meaning GMs, who then, in other discussions, wonder why their players are reluctant to play &quot;the proper way&quot; (refraining from picking up character-defining ratings in skills their character concept does not mandate, investing into merits that make their characters vulnerable, taking actions in play that would fit their character but know would endanger them, et cetera).<br /> <br /> ...At this point, the point I'm trying to make is somewhat running away from me, I'm sorry...<br /> <br /> It would be easy to say something radical like &quot;and from this, we can surmise that the philosophy of 'actions have consequences' is poison even when applied by well-meaning Game Masters&quot;. Or even something like that, but with the addition of &quot;...unless great care is taken&quot;. But that would be wrong. Consistency is important. <i>Verisimilitude </i>is important. These are upheld by enforcing consequences.<br /> <br /> However, at the same time, consequences are on a spectrum. Some are believable, but don't produce fun gameplay. Others help the game move more smoothly, but at the cost of rubbing against the fictional world's believability. But at the same time, you also want to teach your players that <i>it's possible to recover from mistakes</i>. And you have to be mindful of pacing, and the fact that your players will interpret things differently than you do, and...<br /> <br /> ...The point is that games are <i>elastic</i>, tradeoffs have to be weighed against one another, and while realistic consequences are important, they're not necessarily <i>the </i>most important thing to uphold in play. 'Actions have consequences' is one of those memes that have to be applied carefully, even when one means well.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm pretty sure what my players want out of me as a GM is essentially a glorified Consequences Simulator Robot. Like...they just really enjoy playing well-meaning-but-terrible magic garbage people who just keep getting themselves and the world into worse and worse trouble. The phrase, &quot;You guys, this is why the Usurpation happened&quot; is uttered often at my table.<br /> <br /> ...I love my players.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>So I'm making <a href=\"http://godkickingboot.smackjeeves.com/\" target=\"_blank\">God-Kicking Boot</a>, an Exalted webcomic, now. Updates on Sundays. Full-color, mediocre but slowly improving art. It's a thing.<br /> <br /> The absence of a monument can, in its own way, be something of a monument also.</i> -Roger Zelazny","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/512-wise-old-guru","name":"Wise Old Guru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar512_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1097"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-05-05T20:08:39-07:00","id":1215665,"title":"","content":"One thing I've heard---and apologies if this has been said already---is you do kind of figure out a storyline. That is, you figure out a storyline of what would happen if the PCs weren't there. The PCs are there, of course, and everyone tries to deal with the consequences of that.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8026-beans","name":"Beans","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8026_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"472"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-05-05T23:21:57-07:00","id":1215701,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wise Old Guru</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1211728-how-do-you-design-a-story?p=1215661#post1215661\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> The phrase, &quot;You guys, this is why the Usurpation happened&quot; is uttered often at my table.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>&quot;We are the reason the Wyld Hunt is justified&quot; is a common one at my table","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13031-clophiroth","name":"Clophiroth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13031_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"293"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-05-07T08:06:10-07:00","id":1216130,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wise Old Guru</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1211728-how-do-you-design-a-story?p=1215661#post1215661\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The phrase, &quot;You guys, this is why the Usurpation happened&quot; is uttered often at my table.</div> </div> </div> </div>That might be one of the single funniest things I've ever read! #dying<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Co-Host of <a href=\"http://www.fivepoles.com/podcast\" target=\"_blank\">The Deliberative Podcast</a><br /> Follow us on <a href=\"http://www.twitter.com/deliberativepod\" target=\"_blank\">Twitter</a>!<br /> Follow us on <a href=\"http://www.facebook.com/thedeliberativepodcast\" target=\"_blank\">Facebook</a>!<br /> Follow us on the <a href=\"http://www.fivepoles.com\" target=\"_blank\">Web</a>!<br /> Like what we do? Support us on <a href=\"http://patreon.com/thedeliberative\" target=\"_blank\">Patreon</a>!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4164-lukkychukky","name":"Lukkychukky","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4164_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"126"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-05-09T08:24:41-07:00","id":1217000,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wise Old Guru</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1211728-how-do-you-design-a-story?p=1215661#post1215661\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I'm pretty sure what my players want out of me as a GM is essentially a glorified Consequences Simulator Robot. Like...they just really enjoy playing well-meaning-but-terrible magic garbage people who just keep getting themselves and the world into worse and worse trouble. The phrase, &quot;You guys, this is why the Usurpation happened&quot; is uttered often at my table.<br /> <br /> ...I love my players.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I'm with you. I can see how &quot;actions have consequences&quot; could be taken to douchebag extremes, but really it isn't about punishing the players, it's about challenging them. And for Solars especially the idea is sort of baked in. You have all this godlike power and if you really wanted to cut loose you could throw a bucket of dice at the problem and do all these cool tricks and steamroll the opposition and have the Wyld Hunt on your tail inside of a month.<br /> <br /> So when darkness descends on your home, do you take a stand to save your people, and to hell with the consequences? Or do you do only what you can without drawing attention, despite the consequences? Because either way, there will be consequences. If you have wisdom, foresight, or good fortune, you get to pick them. If not, well, you're already here to roll dice, right? Player choices should matter. And for them to matter, sometimes they have to be a <i>bitch</i>.<br /> <br /> That doesn't mean the players won't do something clever and amazing and take a third option, because they often will. In fact, appreciating that their actions have consequences for their characters and the things that are important to those characters will spur them to come up with more creative solutions. By considering the context in which their actions occur, they will become more engaged with the setting. And when they navigate that context successfully, you give them the win. And when they achieve their goals and yet don't (or can't) navigate that context, you still give them the win ... <i>but</i>. <br /> <br /> And that <i>but</i> is what generates your next plot arc. Seriously, &quot;actions have consequences&quot; is the engine that powers half my campaign. I've never had a problem with my players munchkining as a result of this. I mean, the Dawn is a terrifying combat monster, but that's because she's an Essence 5 Dawn and you have to be trying not to be terrifying in combat at that point. The player also trained Integrity up from 0 to 5 and dipped heavily into the Integrity tree despite it not being a favoured skill, and bought Conviction (2.5E) up from 1 to 3, as part of his portrayal of her path to redemption. She went from being an Abyssal-without-a-cause who wavered between wanting rid of her tainted magic and reveling in the addictive rush of dark power, to a hero with a mission and certainty in her path. <br /> <br /> What's great is that even after achieving redemption and becoming a Solar, she's still a hot mess of emotional issues with a spotty record of impulse control who sometimes makes stupendously bad calls, because guess what else? Consequences are <i>fun</i>. I agree that you shouldn't use consequences to punish players <i>as players. </i>But if players are munchkining in an effort to avoid bad consequences for their characters, it means they don't trust the GM to make those consequences fun and interesting.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Measure of Hope is right about everything.&quot; - Wise Old Guru<br /> <br /> Currently running an Exalted 2.5 Abyssals game in a homebrew modern shard because I value neither my time or my sanity, and I'm loving almost every minute of it.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23907-measure-of-hope","name":"Measure of Hope","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"190"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-05-11T19:58:32-07:00","id":1217871,"title":"","content":"I have always thought that the section entitled \u201cBasic Story Structures\u201d on page 241 of the first edition of Changeling: The Lost was the absolute best guide on creating a story that I have ever read. It\u2019s so simple and effective. In my mind, that one section is worth the price of the book.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Host of <a href=\"http://www.fivepoles.com/podcast/\" target=\"_blank\">The Deliberative Podcast</a><br /> Host of <a href=\"https://tca.edennian.com\" target=\"_blank\">Trinity Continuum Airwaves</a><br /> Original Creator of the complete <a href=\"https://www.fantasygrounds.com/forums/showthread.php?9238-Rules-Lite-New-World-of-Darkness&amp;highlight=\" target=\"_blank\">nWoD conversion of Fantasy Grounds 2</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12730-pneumapilot","name":"PneumaPilot","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12730_4.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"149"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-05-12T03:47:26-07:00","id":1217923,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Beans</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1211728-how-do-you-design-a-story?p=1215665#post1215665\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">One thing I've heard---and apologies if this has been said already---is you do kind of figure out a storyline. That is, you figure out a storyline of what would happen if the PCs weren't there. The PCs are there, of course, and everyone tries to deal with the consequences of that.</div> </div> </div> </div>I kind of agree with this, sometimes.<br /> <br /> <br /> A lot of people have talked about a sandbox. And that's certainly one way to do it that can work well: the PCs turn up in an area, and you design all the societies and NPCs. They investigate, and decide what they want to do. This can work very well (and is something Exalted does better than a lot of other games). This does require the player characters to all be on the same page, however: I often see PCs start out well together, wanting to overthrow the evil King X, but then fall apart when it comes to deciding what to replace King X with.<br /> Obviously, this also does require an evil King X. PCs need a reason to want to change the societies; there needs to be issues and problems that make them want to take action. Maybe they feel for the oppressed class of slaves, or they discover that the Vizier is going to summon a horde of demons to conquer the region.<br /> <br /> The other way, is, as someone mentioned earlier, that you have an area the PCs want to protect, but something bad is going to happen and they must stop it. This goes with Beans' approach; the plot is that Big Bad Y is going to conquer the region and do Bad Stuff. Big Bad Y has a plan; first sending spies, then trying to turn regional powers against each other, before finally invading. The PCs' job is to change the story by foiling the plan.<br /> They could, of course, do this by getting a McGuffin, if that's what you want to run.<br /> <br /> These days, I try to run something that's in the middle of these two. The first can lack direction and lead to PCs spending too much time arguing; the second can fall apart due to Exalts' power levels.<br /> <br /> <br /> So, for the 3rd ed game I ran for nearly 2 years, I started out with the PCs' friend being kidnapped, and they had to go rescue him and stop the evil sorcerer. This plot got the PCs working together... mostly (it wasn't perfect). It also allowed me to put plot seeds for three different possible sets of antagonists. It then became a sandbox, with the PCs deciding what to go after... and that leads to the law of consequences. They dealt with nearby kingdoms, changing them into puppets, and then moved against one set of antagonists. The circle of Solars the PCs moved against struck back (at which point it goes back to them foiling the opposing story, of conquering their lands) and so it moved back to more of a plot-driven game.<br /> <br /> I find both approaches have strengths and weaknesses, so you need some of both.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-05-12T05:51:23-07:00","id":1217937,"title":"","content":"Another thing that's important, though: Don't fill <i>everything</i> in. Leave enough room in the setting for the players to feel like it's their world, too. <i>Especially</i> if one or more of the PCs has a high Lore, but don't let the absence of such a PC deprive your group of this.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-05-12T06:06:10-07:00","id":1217944,"title":"","content":"Yes, definitely. I also think it's good to ask people to fill in their own country and culture's details (it makes them feel invested, and helps you with the world-building, though of course you need to be a little careful to ensure it fits), and give ideas for any particular plot they want to do.<br /> <br /> For example, right now the plot we're doing is stopping an evil Nephwrack who has a bunch of insane death-monks and spectre servants, is doing weird rituals, and is clearly up to something bad against the country we live in.<br /> This... is just my own PC's plot. As someone who'd come to join an already existing group, my backstory was &quot;I come from a far-away monastery, where my friends were all murdered by a Nephwrack. I've come to learn kung-fu from the group's kung-fu master, so one day I can return and get revenge.&quot; The ST's obviously decided to roll with it, as we started discovering evidence of some kind of death-cult with links to local mysticism but also my own land's traditions. Eventually, we discovered that the local cult is working for my old nemesis. Obviously I'm the most invested in it, so I don't know what the other players think, but I think it's worked very well.<br /> As the Big Bad is threatening the local lands, the PCs who live there want to stop him, and for me its my own PC's overall motivation.<br /> And it's kept us together despite the fact my PC actually dislikes most of the other PCs.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":2402,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","user":"The Wizard of Oz","date":". 05-12-2018, 09:18 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-05-13T04:27:11-07:00","id":1218171,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>aluminiumtrioxid</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1211728-how-do-you-design-a-story?p=1212620#post1212620\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">'Actions have consequences' is, indeed, a thing I've heard often, but my experience is a bit different than yours - I've mostly heard it from well-meaning GMs, who then, in other discussions, wonder why their players are reluctant to play &quot;the proper way&quot; (refraining from picking up character-defining ratings in skills their character concept does not mandate, investing into merits that make their characters vulnerable, taking actions in play that would fit their character but know would endanger them, et cetera).</div> </div> </div> </div>I've got a lot of &quot;that guy&quot; stories where metagaming lead to a player breaking character to attack an NPC based on something they only knew from the books which really makes a response to it blur the line between in-character consequence and out of character punishment.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Sword of Creation</a> a hub for Exalted related content</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","name":"Lioness","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7_2.png","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5443"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-05-13T06:19:30-07:00","id":1218189,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lioness</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1211728-how-do-you-design-a-story?p=1218171#post1218171\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I've got a lot of &quot;that guy&quot; stories\u2026</div> </div> </div> </div>(shudders)<br /> <br /> You have my sympathies.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}